ar so yeah it was a pretty bad  performance by all accounts for many  Harry Potter fans the fact that Hermione  Granger ended up with Ron Weasley and  not Harry was an unsatisfying way to end  the characters story Harry Potter and  the Deathly Hallows Part 1 did its part  to muddy those waters featuring an  intimate scene between Harry and  Hermione while Ron is off looking for  Salazar Slytherin 'he's locket Horcrux  in order to make a despondent Hermione  buck up for a minute Harry asks her to  dance and they hip and dip around  awkwardly - Nick caves haunting song Oh  children the actors look incredibly  uncomfortable not just dancing with each  other but on their own and no amount of  fancy camerawork could have helped them  pull off the scene if it weren't for  Nick caves vocals and beautiful lyrics  the moment would have been a complete  disaster  even as it foreshadows Ron's eventual  jealous blowup about Harry and Hermione  'he's possibly romantic feelings for  each other Quentin Tarantino has a long  history of inserting himself into his  movies and Django Unchained is arguably  one of his worst cameo moments this  antebellum  revisionist history features Jamie  Foxx's Django an escaped slave assisted  by dentist turned bounty hunter dr. King  Schultz out to wreak bloody vengeance on  slavers including Calvin Candie who has  kidnapped his love Hildy Bunn shaft as  Django's rampage heats up the movie cuts  to a flashback in which Tarantino  appears speaking in a dodgy Australian  accent before Django kills him that  completely derails the climax of the  film yahoo news UK suggested that  Tarantino sported an Australian accent  because he couldn't do a passable  southern one which begs the question of  why II was even included in the movie at  all other than as director sized ego  that is sometimes there's nothing even  the best actors can do when the script  veers into overwrought territory which  is unfortunately the case with  interstellar christopher nolan story  about the intersection of science  technology and family as well as  wormholes and event horizons  since nolan can't make a movie without a  mind-bending twist or two sadly the  audience is well yanked out at the  complicated but well-crafted story when  Anne Hathaway's character Emilia brand  rattles off a Hallmark greeting card of  a monologue that sounds considerably  less than scientific love is the one  thing we're capable of perceiving that  transcends dimensions of time and space  this line comes as brand makes her case  that love is a quantifiable force in the  universe that can actually be used as a  tool of measurement a pretty ridiculous  concept even though in the logic of the  movie she turns out to be right but just  because the movie makes the concept real  doesn't mean that this speech  introducing it works and even Hathaways  considerable talent can't make the  monologue sound anything but forced  thankfully the scene is only two minutes  of pure cheddar and an otherwise  compelling sci-fi drama but it's also  enough to stick out it's one of the  worst acted scenes of the past decade  three Americans are hailed as heroes for  foiling an attack on a high-speed train  in Europe it was a real-life event that  made news around the globe three  American men traveling from Amsterdam to  Paris  Thornton a would-be terrorist mass  shooter through their own quick thinking  and selfless bravery Spencer stone  Anthony Sadler and Alek skarlatos  wrestled the weapon from the gunman and  saved the life of one of his victims  American Marco galleon who was the first  to identify the terrorists later French  authorities found the shooter had been  armed with 300 rounds of bullets more  than enough to have killed most of the  people on the train had he not been  stopped Clint Eastwood directed the 5/17  to Paris and in a surprising move  decided to cast the four american  survivors including the gunshot victim  ooh galleon as themselves  unfortunately the experiment didn't  exactly pay off in this big-budget  Hollywood recreation of real events  these men are heroes and did an  incredible service for their fellow  passengers but they're not professional  actors and it's very clear to audiences  [Music]  the 2010s saw Nicolas Cage and a number  of dreadful movies but Drive angry  stands out as one of the worst the actor  delivers a furious but empty turn is  John Milton a man who has been dead ten  years and returns to earth from Hell in  order to save his infant granddaughter  from being sacrificed to Satan his  one-liners fall so flat that you'd think  an ACME truck rolled over them nothing  of this earth can kill me not of this  earth it's hard to pick a least favorite  scene the absurd gunfight sex scene  Milton peeing fire cage rebounded in  later years with more critically  acclaimed fare like the gonzo Mandy but  for a decent chunk of the decade it  seemed like he may have permanently lost  his way and Drive angry is a perfect  example of why check out one of our  newest videos right here plus even more  looper videos about your favorite movies  and TV shows are coming soon subscribe  to our YouTube channel and hit the bell  so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

